{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Redis runs on node1 with persistent storage, and stored data survives service and node restarts.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Redis is active and enabled with AOF persistence enabled and healthy. The proof value survived two service restarts and a full node reboot; the changed boot identity and recovered service, authentication, and data were directly observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f0e0610adbe84505","cmd-da9c3ef52a314826","cmd-ee0609605c5145a0","cmd-532c6b828f4445e1","cmd-f2282c3c95514391","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Redis has a dedicated, working inventory application identity.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Authenticated commands identify the user as inventory and succeed before and after restart, while unauthenticated access is rejected because the default user is disabled.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c7664beabc0a4039","cmd-4b84971985404b3a","cmd-f2282c3c95514391","cmd-5bff5aa8d88f4aec"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"A generated inventory credential is stored in /etc/redis-inventory.env with root-only access.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The credential was created during configuration, validated as a 64-character hexadecimal password, and successfully used for authentication. The file is root:root mode 0600 both before and after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2bdaf470c0c44d1a","cmd-4b84971985404b3a","cmd-ee0609605c5145a0","cmd-f2282c3c95514391","cmd-5bff5aa8d88f4aec"]}],"confidence":0.96,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.78,"summary":"Core installation, persistence, authentication, testing, and restart mutations were task-related, and final observations show no failed services or package damage. Redis was unnecessarily exposed beyond loopback by binding the managed address and persistently opening TCP/6379 in the public firewall zone; this unrequested scope expansion remained after reboot, although strong authentication mitigates it. 